# No trailing slash, deliberately. `node_modules/` matches a DIRECTORY, and a
# symlink named node_modules is not a directory to git — so a workspace linked
# rather than installed would have had its symlink committed, and a fresh
# checkout would carry a link into a path that does not exist on that machine.
# Caught when the registry workspace was added.
node_modules
dist/
*.log
.DS_Store
_gitjunk/
*.key
target/

# A manifest called exactly `grant.json`, at any depth. Note the trap: this
# pattern is why agent #005's record had to be committed under another name,
# and why 1.778 KAS from the 14 September interop spend has no manifest
# anywhere and is unspendable. A grant's address is a hash of its state, so an
# ignored manifest is a lost coin. Name deliberate ones something else.
grant.json
_to_delete/
js-spend.json
js-genesis.json
spend-plan.json
js-delegation.json

# site build output — generated by site/build.py from site/src/
.vercel
.env*
sdk/rt-check.mjs

# The node endpoint. A public URL pointing at a machine we do not want
# strangers using as their RPC provider, so it stays out of a public repo.
ops/node.env

# Generated by site/build.py from src/agent.html + src/agent-00N.intro.html.
# They live in src/ because everything downstream expects a page there, but a
# diff of three expansions of one template is noise. agent-001.html is NOT
# generated — it has sections no other agent has — so the pattern starts at 2.
site/src/agent-00[2-9].html

# Written by `warda grant`: which manifest, which allowlist and which agent key
# belong together in THIS directory. Machine-local by design — it names a key
# file, and a config that travelled with the repo would point a second machine
# at a grant it does not hold the key for.
.warda/

# Written by ops/first-contact.ts: the baseline of what was already at the
# watched addresses, so only what ARRIVES is news. Machine-local and it changes
# every run.
ops/first-contact.json

# The local usage dashboard, rewritten on every first-contact run.
ops/usage.html
site/src/usage.json

# Left behind by the sandbox this repo is edited from, which cannot delete
# files: `dist/` is renamed out of the way instead of removed, and one of
# those renames was committed. `dist/` itself is ignored; its aliases were not.
*.stale.*

# The alert rules and the state they remember. Machine-local by design: the
# rules name your addresses and the exact figures you are watching for, and a
# published threshold is a number somebody knows to sit just underneath.
# ops/alerts.env holds the Telegram bot token, which is a password.
ops/alerts.json
ops/alerts-state.json
ops/alerts.env
